  body: [
  "The price of peace is not the same for everyone. A reopened Strait of Hormuz could reduce costs for oil importers, shipping firms and households while narrowing price advantages for producers selling outside the Gulf. Yet Gulf exporters could benefit from restored volumes even if benchmark prices decline. The net effect depends on barrels actually shipped, realized prices, discounts, transport costs and the timing of sanctions relief.",
  "What changed this week. On November 6, Reuters reported Brent crude heading for an approximately 16 percent weekly fall as markets priced in greater hopes of de-escalation following U.S. midterm elections. At the same time, Reuters reported tanker incidents in and around Hormuz and no final peace agreement. Falling futures prices are not evidence that commercial shipping has normalized.",
  "The negotiating trade-off. Reuters reported that negotiators have explored phased arrangements linking Iranian reopening of the waterway to U.S. relief from its blockade. Iran seeks sanctions and financial relief; Washington seeks reliable navigation and security concessions. Gulf governments oppose arrangements that institutionalize Iranian tolls or unilateral control. Negotiating proposals are not agreed terms.",
  "Oil importers and consumers. China, India, Japan, South Korea and other energy importers could gain from reduced supply-risk premiums and lower transport costs. American drivers might see relief in fuel prices, but retail changes lag wholesale markets and depend on refining margins, taxes, inventories and regional supply. Lower crude prices do not translate into equal savings for every household.",
  "Gulf producers: volume versus price. For Saudi Arabia, Iraq, Kuwait, Qatar and the UAE, reopening could restore sales channels and reduce costly workarounds. But increased supply could also pressure global prices. A producer's revenue equals realized price multiplied by sold volume; profit additionally depends on operating, shipping and financing costs. Without consistent country-level figures, a single winner ranking would be misleading.",
  "Russia and other alternative suppliers. Producers that benefited from disruption-related price premiums could lose some pricing advantage if Gulf exports recover. Russia's actual position would depend on export volumes, sanctions, discounts, freight and customers, not Brent alone. Reopening could also ease inflation and weaken a source of leverage associated with the conflict.",
  "Shipping and insurance: the first practical test. Even with a political agreement, carriers need credible security guarantees, mine-risk assessment, available crews and ships, and insurable routes. The International Union of Marine Insurance has said war cover may remain available on negotiated voyage-specific terms, with risk continually reassessed. Insurance costs and transit volumes may recover at different speeds.",
  "Iran's economic calculation. Iran could regain export revenue and financial access under a settlement, but the extent depends on the exact U.S. blockade and sanctions provisions, compliance and the capacity of damaged or disrupted infrastructure. Iran may also lose coercive leverage over the chokepoint. Relief for Iranian civilians cannot be assumed to arrive immediately or evenly.",
  "Three outcomes to measure. Scenario one: a credible comprehensive reopening reduces shipping risks and supports higher Gulf export volumes, while putting downward pressure on the oil-risk premium. Scenario two: a limited or conditional corridor restores some shipments but retains delays, insurance surcharges and geopolitical uncertainty. Scenario three: talks fail and attacks continue, sustaining disruptions and possible price spikes. These are conditional scenarios, not predictions.",
  "The EP News verification standard. To calculate winners and losers responsibly, compare the same time period and definitions for tanker passages, oil and LNG export volumes, realized export prices, insurance rates, freight charges and import bills. Distinguish oil-price movement from physical supply recovery, government revenue from company profit, and announced agreements from ships safely transiting. As of November 6, reopening remains unconfirmed."
],
